The lifecycle changes contain two process-reuse races that can signal unrelated processes, and the forced legacy recovery is gated out for the real paired-record upgrade case. These are correctness and safety defects in the core behavior introduced by the patch.

Full review comments:

- [P1] Open the pidfd before trusting the birth token — /var/home/matt/dev/mybd/.worktrees/beads/review-psxg5-proxy-identity/internal/procid/procid_linux.go:63-72
  When the verified process exits and its PID is reused between `Verify` and `pidfdOpen`, the returned fd targets the replacement process while `Handle.token` still describes the original; the pidfd `Signal` path performs no further token check and can kill that unrelated replacement. Open the pidfd first and validate that its process still corresponds to `tok` before returning the handle.

- [P1] Support paired legacy records during forced recovery — /var/home/matt/dev/mybd/.worktrees/beads/review-psxg5-proxy-identity/internal/storage/dbproxy/proxy/shutdown.go:63-67
  When upgrading a running pre-v2 managed-local proxy, both `proxy.pid` and `proxy-child.pid` are legacy records, so `Shutdown` returns `ErrUnverifiableProcess` in both `proxyErr` and `backendErr`. This predicate then rejects the force path because `backendErr != nil`, meaning `bd dolt stop --force` cannot perform its advertised legacy recovery for an actual pre-upgrade topology; the paired legacy backend outcome needs to be handled rather than gating recovery out.

- [P2] Hold a stable process handle across force-stop verification — /var/home/matt/dev/mybd/.worktrees/beads/review-psxg5-proxy-identity/internal/storage/dbproxy/proxy/force_stop.go:175-180
  During `--force`, if the matched `bd`/`dolt` process exits and its PID is recycled after `processExecutableBasename` but before `FindProcess`/`Kill`, this code signals the replacement without checking its executable. That violates the force-stop executable-match guarantee and can kill an unrelated process under PID churn; retain a stable OS process handle or birth identity across the executable check and signal.

- [P2] Treat an already-exited pidfd target as stopped — /var/home/matt/dev/mybd/.worktrees/beads/review-psxg5-proxy-identity/internal/procid/procid_linux.go:88-90
  If the target exits naturally after `Open` but before a fatal signal, `PidfdSendSignal` can return `ESRCH`; unlike the fallback path, this branch reports that as a shutdown failure. This race can make `bd dolt stop` fail and leave the stale record behind even though the process is already gone, so `ESRCH` should be accepted for fatal signals.
